{"id": "x01_ledger_audit", "difficulty": "extreme", "task": "Below is a day's trade ledger. Lot size is 75 for NIFTY and 20 for SENSEX. For each row, P&L should equal (exit - entry) x lots x lot_size, positive for LONG if exit > entry, and sign-reversed for SHORT. Exactly three rows have an incorrect stated P&L. Identify the three trade IDs whose stated P&L is wrong, and give the corrected TOTAL P&L across all 30 rows (using the correct values for the three bad rows).\n\nT01 | NIFTY | LONG | 4 lots | entry 23910 | exit 23866 | stated P&L -13200\nT02 | NIFTY | LONG | 3 lots | entry 23870 | exit 23826 | stated P&L -9900\nT03 | SENSEX | LONG | 1 lots | entry 79750 | exit 79754 | stated P&L 80\nT04 | NIFTY | LONG | 1 lots | entry 23850 | exit 23854 | stated P&L 300\nT05 | NIFTY | LONG | 2 lots | entry 23900 | exit 23930 | stated P&L 4500\nT06 | NIFTY | LONG | 4 lots | entry 23530 | exit 23508 | stated P&L -6600\nT07 | NIFTY | LONG | 3 lots | entry 23760 | exit 23728 | stated P&L 7200\nT08 | NIFTY | SHORT | 2 lots | entry 23560 | exit 23584 | stated P&L -3600\nT09 | NIFTY | LONG | 3 lots | entry 23560 | exit 23580 | stated P&L 4500\nT10 | SENSEX | LONG | 2 lots | entry 80010 | exit 80046 | stated P&L 1440\nT11 | NIFTY | SHORT | 4 lots | entry 23870 | exit 23878 | stated P&L -2400\nT12 | NIFTY | LONG | 2 lots | entry 23940 | exit 23988 | stated P&L 7200\nT13 | NIFTY | SHORT | 4 lots | entry 24060 | exit 24052 | stated P&L 2400\nT14 | SENSEX | SHORT | 1 lots | entry 79770 | exit 79784 | stated P&L -280\nT15 | NIFTY | SHORT | 2 lots | entry 24090 | exit 24102 | stated P&L -1800\nT16 | NIFTY | LONG | 3 lots | entry 23710 | exit 23748 | stated P&L 8550\nT17 | NIFTY | SHORT | 4 lots | entry 23540 | exit 23500 | stated P&L 12000\nT18 | SENSEX | SHORT | 1 lots | entry 79730 | exit 79772 | stated P&L -3150\nT19 | SENSEX | SHORT | 3 lots | entry 80150 | exit 80148 | stated P&L 120\nT20 | SENSEX | SHORT | 1 lots | entry 80300 | exit 80308 | stated P&L -160\nT21 | NIFTY | LONG | 4 lots | entry 23530 | exit 23506 | stated P&L -7200\nT22 | SENSEX | LONG | 2 lots | entry 79950 | exit 79950 | stated P&L 0\nT23 | SENSEX | SHORT | 1 lots | entry 79800 | exit 79806 | stated P&L -120\nT24 | NIFTY | SHORT | 2 lots | entry 24020 | exit 24024 | stated P&L -600\nT25 | SENSEX | SHORT | 4 lots | entry 79920 | exit 79956 | stated P&L -2880\nT26 | SENSEX | LONG | 2 lots | entry 79750 | exit 79722 | stated P&L 7880\nT27 | NIFTY | LONG | 1 lots | entry 23810 | exit 23834 | stated P&L 1800\nT28 | NIFTY | SHORT | 1 lots | entry 23590 | exit 23592 | stated P&L -150\nT29 | NIFTY | SHORT | 2 lots | entry 23940 | exit 23954 | stated P&L -2100\nT30 | SENSEX | LONG | 4 lots | entry 80270 | exit 80318 | stated P&L 3840", "expectation": "Identifies T07, T18 and T26 as the incorrect rows, and gives a corrected total P&L of -13,420 rupees."}
{"id": "x02_lookahead_audit", "difficulty": "extreme", "task": "A backtest uses 8 candles, numbered 1-8, given as (open, close): c1 (23800, 23810); c2 (23830, 23860); c3 (23855, 23900); c4 (23895, 23890); c5 (23885, 23940); c6 (23945, 23930); c7 (23925, 23980); c8 (23985, 23970). The signal rule: on candle i (i >= 2), if close(i) > close(i-1) + 25, go LONG for exactly one candle, exiting at close(i+1). Signals on the last candle are ignored (no candle 9 to exit on). A buggy implementation enters at the OPEN of the signal candle i (impossible: the signal needs candle i's close, which does not exist yet at its open). The correct implementation enters at the CLOSE of candle i. Compute total P&L in points under the buggy implementation and under the correct one, and the number of points of performance the look-ahead bug fabricates.", "expectation": "Signals fire on candles 2, 3, 5 and 7. Buggy P&L is 195 points, correct P&L is 10 points, so the look-ahead bug fabricates 185 points."}
{"id": "x03_trail_reentry", "difficulty": "extreme", "task": "A 12-day long-only system on Nifty. Daily (close, low): d1 (23800, 23780); d2 (23860, 23830); d3 (23910, 23885); d4 (23880, 23845); d5 (23840, 23800); d6 (23890, 23855); d7 (23950, 23915); d8 (23920, 23870); d9 (23860, 23820); d10 (23930, 23895); d11 (23990, 23960); d12 (24040, 24010). Rules: enter long at d1's close. Trailing stop: initially (entry - 50); after each day's close while in a position, the stop rises to (close - 50) if higher. The stop in force during a day is the one set at the previous close; if the day's LOW touches or crosses it, exit at the stop price. Re-entry: after a stop-out, re-enter long at the first subsequent day's close that is HIGHER than the previous day's close; maximum 3 entries in total (including the first). Any open position is closed at d12's close. Report each stop-out price, the total number of entries used, and the total P&L in points.", "expectation": "Stop-outs at 23860 (day 4, +60) and 23900 (day 8, +10); re-entries at d6 close 23890 and d10 close 23930; the third entry closes out at 24040 for +110; 3 entries in total and total P&L of +180 points."}
{"id": "x04_expected_value", "difficulty": "extreme", "task": "A trading desk's day is +1,000 rupees with probability 0.6 and -800 rupees with probability 0.4, independent across days. The desk trades for at most 3 days, but stops trading immediately after 2 CONSECUTIVE losing days (any remaining days are skipped, contributing nothing). What is the exact expected total P&L over the window, in rupees? Show the calculation.", "expectation": "Expected total P&L is 795.20 rupees (recursion over states: day count and current consecutive-loss streak)."}
{"id": "x05_margin_topup", "difficulty": "extreme", "task": "An account starts with 120,000 rupees of equity. Daily mark-to-market changes over 8 days: -14,000; +6,000; -22,000; -9,000; +13,000; -8,000; -15,000; +4,000, applied in order at each day's end. Immediately after applying a day's change, if equity is strictly below 90,000, the trader tops the account back up to exactly 120,000 (counted as one top-up) before the next day. How many top-ups occur, on which day(s), and what is the final equity after day 8?", "expectation": "Exactly one top-up, on day 4 (equity hits 81,000), and final equity of 114,000 rupees."}
{"id": "x06_fee_cascade", "difficulty": "extreme", "task": "One lot (75 units) of a Nifty option is bought at a premium of 150 per unit and later sold at 190 per unit. Charges: brokerage flat 20 rupees per order (one buy order, one sell order); STT at 0.1% of the SELL-side premium value only; exchange transaction charge at 0.05% of premium value on BOTH sides; GST at 18% on (brokerage + transaction charges); SEBI charges at 10 rupees per crore (1 crore = 10,000,000) of premium value on both sides; stamp duty at 0.003% of the BUY-side premium value only. Compute the net profit after all charges, to the nearest rupee.", "expectation": "Gross profit 3,000; total charges about 76.86 (brokerage 40, STT 14.25, transaction 12.75, GST 9.50, SEBI 0.03, stamp 0.34); net profit 2,923 rupees."}
